  "body": "The dashboard's **Update** button stopped halfway: it downloaded, verified and installed/staged the release, then told the operator to restart manually. This closes that lifecycle.\n\n## Why the existing restart callback was not enough\n\nFCC already had an automatic restart callback for config changes. It rebuilds the ASGI application inside the **same Python interpreter**. That is correct for a port/config change and wrong for a package update: `sys.modules` still holds the old bytecode, so it would look like a restart while continuing to run the old release.\n\nThis PR adds a separate **process replacement** path and leaves config restart semantics unchanged.\n\n## What happens now\n\n### Linux, macOS and normal WSL/ext4\n\n1. Download and SHA-256 verify the GitHub release wheel.\n2. Preserve the uv receipt's Python pin and extras.\n3. Install through fixed argv (`uv tool install --force`).\n4. Return the successful HTTP response to the dashboard.\n5. Gracefully close the complete runtime ownership graph.\n6. Flush Loguru's `enqueue=True` queue and clean up registered children.\n7. `execv()` the stable `fcc-server` launcher outside the replaced uv tool environment, preserving argv, environment, cwd and PID.\n\n### Native Windows\n\nWindows cannot replace a running tool environment: the interpreter and loaded DLLs hold it open. The existing detached PowerShell helper remains the owner of installation:\n\n1. The server stages the verified wheel and helper.\n2. The upgrade response is returned.\n3. The server gracefully closes and exits.\n4. The helper pins the parent by PID **and creation time**, waits for handle release, and retries uv with the existing backoff.\n5. It records success/failure and starts `fcc-server.exe` **only after success**.\n\nA failed or partial install is never relaunched.\n\n### WSL tool directory under `/mnt`\n\nRefused before install. DrvFs may expose Windows file-lock semantics even though Python reports POSIX; attempting in-place replacement can corrupt the environment. The error tells the operator to move `UV_TOOL_DIR` to WSL's filesystem or update after stopping.\n\n## Adjacent bug fixed\n\nThe uv receipt path was hardcoded to `~/.local/share/uv/tools/...`. On Windows the real path is under `%APPDATA%\\uv\\tools`, so dashboard updates silently dropped installed extras and pinned the wrong interpreter. Receipt and launcher roots now come from:\n\n```text\nuv tool dir\nuv tool dir --bin\n```\n\nThat honors platform defaults and `UV_TOOL_DIR`.\n\n## Dashboard behavior\n\nThe button now moves through:\n\n```text\nUpdating… → Restarting — reconnecting… → Updated and restarted on vX.Y.Z\n```\n\nReconnect polling:\n\n- tolerates the expected connection refusal while the port is unbound;\n- ignores a response from the old process;\n- resolves only when the installed version answers;\n- times out with a concrete error after two minutes.\n\nDeferred helper outcomes render exactly once after relaunch, including successful Windows installs, then the receipt is consumed.\n\n## Verification\n\nStatic checks:\n\n```text\nsuppression guard  pass\nruff format        pass\nruff check         pass\nty                 pass\n```\n\nFocused tests:\n\n```text\n48 updater/runtime/supervisor/import-boundary tests  pass\n2 upgrade API tests                                 pass\n```\n\nFull suite against an unmodified `fork/main` worktree in the same shell:\n\n```text\nbranch failures    16\nbaseline failures  16\nnew failures        0\n```\n\nThe 16 are identical pre-existing PowerShell installer/uninstaller cases.\n\n### Real WSL process-image handoff\n\nDisposable uv tool directory, fake HOME, isolated port `8144`; live `8082` untouched:\n\n```text\noriginal PID  10203\nnew command   /tmp/fcc-restart-bin/fcc-server\nlistener      0.0.0.0:8144, PID 10203\nversion       4.53.2\n```\n\nLog:\n\n```text\nRestarting with the updated server...\nStarted server process [10203]\nAdmin UI: http://127.0.0.1:8144/admin\nApplication startup complete.\n```\n\nThe old runtime closed, logs flushed, the process image changed, the same PID rebound, and the new release served requests.\n\n### Browser reconnect state machine\n\nThe real page function received this sequence:\n\n```text\ncall 1  v4.53.1 (old process)  → ignore\ncall 2  connection refused     → retry\ncall 3  connection refused     → retry\ncall 4  v4.53.2                → resolve\n```\n\nResolved after 2.5 seconds with no error banner.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- Native Windows helper relaunch is pinned structurally and by the existing helper contract tests, but this PR has not yet been exercised as a published N-1 → N dashboard update against an isolated Windows uv installation. That must be the release acceptance test before the live WSL install.\n- A streaming request still active after Uvicorn's five-second graceful-shutdown budget may be cut. The server does not splice or replay committed output during an update.\n- WSL installations deliberately configured under `/mnt` require moving the uv tool root or a stopped-server install; this release refuses rather than risk corruption.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
