    "message": "fix(updater): restart automatically into the installed release\n\nThe dashboard update button stopped halfway through its own lifecycle. POSIX\ninstalled new files but kept serving old imported bytecode; Windows staged a\nverified wheel for installation after shutdown but never asked the server to\nclose and never relaunched it. The UI then instructed the operator to stop and\nstart fcc-server manually.\n\nA package update cannot reuse the existing restart callback: that callback\nrebuilds the ASGI application inside the same Python interpreter and therefore\ncontinues executing the old modules. This change adds a separate process-\nreplacement request through the admin port, ApplicationRuntime, composition\nroot, and server supervisor. The upgrade endpoint schedules it only after the\nsuccessful HTTP response has been committed.\n\nThe supervisor now returns an explicit STOP, RELOAD, or REPLACE_PROCESS action.\nReplacement is accepted only after RuntimeASGIApp reports its complete ownership\ngraph closed; an incomplete shutdown degrades to STOP instead of leaking old\nproviders, messaging tasks, or CLI sessions into the next image.\n\nOn POSIX, the updater installs in place, flushes Loguru's enqueue=True queue,\nruns best-effort child cleanup, and os.execv()s the stable fcc-server launcher\noutside the uv tool environment with the original argv. On Windows, the old\nprocess exits after the same graceful close; the existing detached PowerShell\nhelper waits for PID + creation-time identity to disappear, retries uv after\nhandle-release races, records the outcome, and starts the stable launcher only\nafter a successful install. It never starts a half-installed environment.\n\nuv's receipt and executable roots are now resolved with `uv tool dir` and\n`uv tool dir --bin`, honoring platform defaults and UV_TOOL_DIR. The old\nhardcoded ~/.local/share path made Windows silently lose installed extras during\nupdates. WSL tool environments under /mnt are rejected before install because\nDrvFs can expose Windows sharing semantics; normal WSL/ext4 uses the proven\nPOSIX exec path.\n\nThe dashboard changes from Updating to Restarting/reconnecting, tolerates the\nexpected disconnect, ignores an old-process response, and resolves only when\nthe expected installed version answers. Deferred helper outcomes are shown once\non the relaunched server and then consumed, rather than leaving a permanent\nfailure banner or silently ignoring success.\n\nVerification:\n\n- suppression guard, ruff format, ruff check, and ty pass\n- 48 focused updater/runtime/supervisor/import-boundary tests pass\n- 2 upgrade API tests pass; failure never schedules replacement\n- full suite matches unmodified fork/main exactly (16 PowerShell script failures\n  in this shell, zero new)\n- real isolated WSL handoff: original PID 10203 became\n  `/tmp/fcc-restart-bin/fcc-server`, rebound :8144 with the same PID, and served\n  v4.53.2; live :8082 was untouched\n- browser reconnect state machine: old v4.53.1 response, two connection errors,\n  then v4.53.2 resolved after four polls; no error banner\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
